ic, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's see strengths/concerns:\n\nA: Anticipate dangerous situations (good decision quality), see complex connections (technical), spatial vision (technical), leadership (crew cooperation). Concerns: show-off (crew cooperation, communication), not open to new ideas (adaptability). No info on stress resilience, reliability.\n\nB: Very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication). So strong reliability and stress, but poor crew cooperation.\n\nC: Correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated tech (technical), difficulty communicating ideas (crew cooperation), egocentric (crew), not willing to further education (adaptability). So good decision and stress, but poor communication and adaptability.\n\nD: Responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), know-it-all (crew cooperation), hot temper (crew cooperation, stress). Mixed.\n\nWe need best overall fit. Likely candidate C has good decision and stress but poor communication. B has reliability and stress but poor crew cooperation. A has technical but unknown reliability and stress, plus show-off. D has many strengths but hot temper and know-it-all.\n\nWhich candidate best balances?